    "OverallSummary": "This case involves Stewart Ginn, a registered representative with Independent Financial Group, LLC, who churned and excessively traded five customer accounts between July 2020 and December 2022. Four of the customers were retail customers covered by Regulation BI's Care Obligation, while the fifth was a profit sharing plan not classified as a retail customer. Ginn's excessive trading resulted in combined realized losses of approximately $2.22 million while generating more than $2.24 million in commissions. \n\nThe Care Obligation violations specifically cited in this case involved excessive trading in the accounts of Customers 1-4. The analytical steps used to determine these violations included: (1) examining the cost-to-equity ratios (ranging from 13.7% to 23.4% annualized) which showed customers needed to earn unrealistic returns just to break even; (2) analyzing the pattern of in-and-out trading where Ginn would frequently buy securities and sell them shortly thereafter with minimal price changes but substantial commissions; (3) considering the customers' investment profiles (three were retired seniors with moderate risk tolerances); and (4) identifying that Ginn exercised de facto control over the accounts. \n\nGinn also executed discretionary trades without written authorization in four accounts, further demonstrating his control. He typically charged 3% commissions on purchases and 2% on sales, which made it virtually impossible for customers to profit. The complaint specifically charges Ginn with willful violations of Exchange Act Rule 15l-1(a)(1) (Regulation BI) for Customers 1-4, and violations of FINRA Rules 2111 and 2010 for Customer 5's excessive trading."
